WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.180
Hey, Mando — we know you're a man of few
words, but can you say whoops?

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:08.340
After the fourth installment of The Mandalorian
hit Disney+, fans took to the internet in

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:12.339
droves to point out that the episode had fallen
prey to one of the oldest, most common boneheaded

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:15.560
errors in filmmaking history: the old boom-mic-in-the-shot
gaffe.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.949
The mistake occurred roughly 16 minutes into
the episode, as the Mandalorian was having

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:24.320
a chat with Omera, the farmer who had given
him refuge during a stop on the planet Sorgan.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.560
Their conversation took place in a rather
dimly lit area, and unless you had your screen's

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.190
brightness turned up pretty high, it was fairly
easy to miss — but once you know it's there,

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:34.320
it's impossible not to see it.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.550
The mic comes creeping in from the top of
the frame, then bobs around a little bit;

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.320
it's even visible from multiple angles.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:41.080
"Oopsie!"

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.600
On Twitter, Mando fans were split pretty evenly
between "Well, this is just inexcusable,"

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.460
and "Geez, cut the crew some slack."

00:00:48.969 --> 00:00:52.399
While it might seem like a series with The
Mandalorian's high profile should be able

00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:56.190
to avoid making one of the most well-worn
production errors in the book, the fact is

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:00.110
that the series is actually produced by human
beings, and darned if human beings don't drop

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:01.380
the ball every now and again.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.440
"Yes, there were a great many dangers involved.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:09.360
But perhaps the most dangerous were the poisonous
fish."

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.880
All in all, it's really not worth pulling
the ears off your gundark; the gaffe basically

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:17.740
amounted to an out-of-place dark blob intruding
on the frame in an area where viewers' eyes

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:18.640
aren't trained to look.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.619
It's not as if an item which clearly had no
place in the show's fictional world was left

00:01:22.619 --> 00:01:26.720
prominently in the frame, as astutely noted
by one Twitter user who wrote, quote, "It's

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.520
still better than the GoT coffee cup."

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.460
And, hey — if it's really shattering your
immersion like so many poorly built Death

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.860
Stars, just pretend it's a droid or something.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:35.900
We present to you…

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.580
BM-MC, the galaxy's first personal audio amplification
droid.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.840
Action figures coming Christmas 2020.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.160
All joking aside, you may be curious as to
whether any other such ridiculous mistakes

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.090
have made it into the Star Wars franchise,
one of the most beloved in the world the films

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:53.920
of which have been dissected and analyzed
frame-by-frame by legions of dedicated fans

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:54.420
for decades.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:55.920
The answer: oh, yes.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:56.420
Oh, God…

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:57.660
Just... so many.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.960
In fact, you may not have realized that one
of the most famous errors in the entire series

00:02:01.969 --> 00:02:06.200
actually was an error, because home video
releases starting with the DVD release have

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.440
made it appear intentional.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.640
In Star Wars Episode IV: A New Hope, when
a trio of Stormtroopers burst into the Death

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.190
Star control room in pursuit of our heroes,
the one on the right just clocks his head

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:18.860
on the door frame; since the sound effects
for the sequence were added in post, there

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.700
was no accompanying klunk when this happened.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.550
After fans caught the mistake, just such a
sound effect was added for the home video

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.260
version but don't let them fool you.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.660
The actor portraying the clumsiest Stormtrooper
in the galaxy absolutely did not mean to give

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.100
himself a concussion while making his big
entrance.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.660
There are plenty more examples to be found
in A New Hope alone, many of them having to

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:41.380
do with C-3PO; it turns out that the shiny,
reflective quality of the droid's dome wasn't

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:45.370
exactly accounted for in a number of shots
in which camera operators and/or cameras are

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:46.060
visible in it.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.020
There are also several instances of crew members
briefly wandering into the frame, and, yes,

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:55.310
one shot corrected for home video releases
in which the boom mic is visible, as Luke

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:58.020
Skywalker arrives at the Mos Eisley cantina
in his landspeeder.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:03.120
MovieMistakes.com lists a whopping 279 errors
in the film par for the course for the movies

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.720
of both the original and prequel trilogies,
which all contain a similar number of mistakes…

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.780
and no, we don't just mean Mr. Binks.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:14.340
Of course, most of these fall within the realm
of simple continuity errors objects jumping

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.010
from one hand to another, or things on a table
rearranging themselves between cuts, for example

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.980
which are common to just about all movies.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.780
But although the sequel flicks have cleaned
up their acts just a bit, it's safe to say

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:29.030
that the Star Wars franchise has a long history
of bloopers and blunders which somehow survived

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.260
the editing process to make it all the way
to the screen.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.200
So, yes, we're inclined to come down on the
side of giving the hardworking guys and gals

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.040
who bring us The Mandalorian, the best new
series on television by virtually any measure,

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:39.540
a break.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:44.080
Unless and until, that is, a future episode
ends up with a shot of a discarded Big Mac

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.480
carton chilling on the control panel of Mando's
ship.

